1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,720
If you've ever woken up unable to move and thought something was wrong with you, you're not alone - and you're not imagining it.

2
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:19,520
English has no everyday word for this experience, so many people go through it without ever hearing it has a name: sleep paralysis, a well-documented part of how REM sleep works, not a sign anything's broken.

3
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,480
Doctors point to a few things that make it more likely.

4
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:25,920
An irregular sleep schedule throws off your REM cycle.

5
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:34,880
Sleeping flat on your back is linked to over four times as many episodes as side sleeping, since it raises airway resistance and disrupts that cycle further.

6
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:43,600
It also shows up more in people dealing with anxiety or other mental health conditions - bedtime tension keeps your nervous system too alert to power down.

7
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,920
None of that means something's wrong with you.

8
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:52,000
A steadier schedule, side sleeping, and managing stress before bed make episodes less frequent.

9
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:59,840
One thing worth flagging to a doctor: if it keeps happening often, since repeated sleep paralysis can sometimes link to narcolepsy.
